Mark a query @live and send it over either subscription transport (graphql-ws or /api/graphql/stream). You get its result at once, then the new result whenever a write changes what it read. POST /api/graphql ignores @live and answers once.
query OpenOrders @live(throttle: 1000) {
  orders(filter: { status: { _eq: "open" } }, sort: ["-date_created"], limit: 20) {
    id
    status
    customer { name }
  }
  orders_metadata(filter: { status: { _eq: "open" } }) { total }
}

// …
What the query read | Re-runs on / A list (root list, nested to-many, _metadata, _aggregated, M2M / M2A links) | Any create, update or delete in that collection — a new or changed row may join the list. / A collection named in a filter (through a relation) | Any write in that collection. / A single record (_by_id, a nested many-to-one) | A write to a record the result held, or to the id the query asked for.
• Every re-run runs as you, never as the person who wrote: your role, row filters, user scopes and API key scopes apply each time, read fresh. If you lose access, the next result carries the errors your query now gets, with no data. If your sign-in ends, you get SESSION_ENDED and the live query completes. • Re-runs are throttled (@live(throttle: ms), 100–60000, default 500) and a burst of writes collapses into one re-run. A result identical to the last one is not sent again. • Writes made by raw SQL produce no event, so they show up only on the next re-run something else triggers. A sort on a related record's field re-sorts only when the sorted collection itself changes. • Limits: 10 open live queries per person (LIVE_QUERY_MAX_PER_USER) and 500 per server (LIVE_QUERY_MAX). A query over the limit gets one result with error code LIVE_QUERY_LIMIT. The query cost limits (GRAPHQL_MAX_SELECTIONS, GRAPHQL_MAX_DEPTH, an API key's own depth cap) apply as on POST /api/graphql. • In multi-tenant hosting a live query hears its own tenant's writes only.

